<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Harran</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Harran is an ancient city in upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, in present-day south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, known as a centre of the moon god Sin. After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a>, the last Assyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashur-uballit-ii">Ashur-uballit II</a> made a final stand there. Its capture by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a> marked the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harran.jpg" alt="Harran" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hamdigumus · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harran_2015.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Harran lies about 40 kilometres south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanliurfa">Urfa</a> in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, at a crossroads between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> and on the border between the ancient Mesopotamian and Anatolian cultures. Its name goes back to the earliest documents that mention it, where it is written Ḫarrānu, meaning journey, caravan or crossroad. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> also called it Huzirina, the Greeks Kárrhai and the Romans Carrhae.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest written records suggest that Harran was founded between about 2500 and 2000 BCE, possibly as a merchant outpost of traders from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>. Tablets from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebla">Ebla</a> of the 24th century BCE name a queen Zugalum as its ruler. Its place on trade routes made it a target for raids. Harran passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a> in the 16th century BCE and was conquered by the Assyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adad-nirari-i">Adad-nirari I</a>. It became a fortified provincial capital second only to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assur">Assur</a>, and its temple of the moon god Sin, the Eḫulḫul, was consulted and renovated by many Mesopotamian rulers. Harran was the last capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-assyrian-empire">Neo-Assyrian Empire</a> from 612 to 609 BCE.</p><p>It then came under the Neo-Babylonian, Achaemenid, Macedonian and Seleucid empires, and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> settled there. In 53 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-carrhae">Battle of Carrhae</a>, one of the worst defeats in Roman history, was fought there. The moon cult lasted long into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, and the city was sometimes called Hellenopolis, city of the pagan Greeks. In the winter of 639-640 the pagans of Harran negotiated its surrender to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun</a> general Iyad ibn Ghanm, who gave them a new moon temple. Harran was briefly the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> from 744 to 750 and again of the Numayrid Emirate from 990 to 1081. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> conquered it in 1260 and largely destroyed it in 1271.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Harran is a district of Şanlıurfa Province. Its old town is largely surrounded by ancient walls about 4.5 kilometres long, which once had 187 bastions. The Harran Castle is a large brick fortress of unknown date. The town is known for its beehive houses, which resemble domed buildings shown in Assyrian reliefs of the 7th century BCE.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Harran” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Medes sacked Assur in 614 BCE, the Babylonians and Medes together destroyed Nineveh in 612 BCE, and the last Assyrian king, Ashur-uballit II, abandoned his refuge at <b>Harran</b> in 610 BCE and, after the fall of Harran, failed with Egyptian help to retake it in 609.</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The turning point was the alliance with Cyaxares of Media, which produced the sack of Nineveh in 612 BCE and, after the fall of <b>Harran</b> in 610–609 BCE, the destruction of Assyrian power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>With Babylonian allies and their siege expertise, the Medes captured fortified cities such as Ashur (614 BCE) and Nineveh (612 BCE), and pursued the Assyrian remnant to <b>Harran</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/median-empire">Median Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Saladin's siege of Mosul in 1182 stalled, Sinjar fell after fifteen days, and the coalition that met him at <b>Harran</b> in February 1183 asked for peace: “like men they advanced, like women they vanished”.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zengid-dynasty">Zengid dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its earliest dated inscriptions, at Zabad in Syria in 512, Jabal Usays in 528 and <b>Harran</b> in 568, show it taking shape in the century before Islam.</blockquote><a href="/language/arabic">Arabic · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>At <b>Harran</b>, the Seljuk emirs of Mosul and Mardin defeated the Crusader states of the Principality of Antioch and the County of Edessa.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-7">7 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
